<header><p class="st-kicker">khanate · Caucasus</p><h1 class="st-title">Kurin Khanate</h1><p class="st-meta">1812 CE – 1864 CE · capital Kurakh</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A khanate created by Russia in the Lezgin lands of southern Dagestan, which lasted until the end of the Caucasian War.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1812 the Russian authorities formed the Kurin Khanate from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lezgins">Lezgin</a> communities in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samur-river">Samur</a> river area of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dagestan">Dagestan</a>, which had been free societies. They placed it under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aslan-khan-of-kazikumukh">Aslan Khan</a>, a member of the Kazikumukh ruling family, as a Russian ally, with its seat at Kurakh.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The khanate governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lezgins">Lezgin</a> villages of farmers and herders under Russian supervision. Its khans held Russian military rank and pensions, collected taxes and supplied troops. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aslan-khan-of-kazikumukh">Aslan Khan</a> also gained the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kazikumukh-khanate">Kazikumukh Khanate</a>, and the two were often ruled together. Kurakh had a Russian fort, and the khanate remained loyal during the wars with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/caucasian-imamate">Caucasian Imamate</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After the defeat of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam-shamil">Shamil</a> and the stabilisation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dagestan">Dagestan</a>, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> abolished the khanate in 1864 and incorporated it into the Kyurin district of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dagestan-oblast">Dagestan Oblast</a>. The area remains the heartland of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lezgins">Lezgin people</a> in southern Dagestan.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kurin-khanate.jpg" alt="Kurin Khanate" width="320" height="210"><figcaption>Image: Томкиев · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kurinskoe_khanstvo_in_the_Map_of_Caucasus_with_the_borders_1801-1813.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1812 CE – 1864 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>khanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kurakh</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Aslan Khan</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Lezgian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kyurin Khanate, Kura Khanate</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1838&amp;focus=kurin-khanate" aria-label="Kurin Khanate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5845 696 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kurin Khanate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6165 952L6487 457"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6487 457h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6165 952h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6165 952h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Kurakh. <a href="/atlas?year=1838&amp;focus=kurin-khanate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

